WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.000
On Oct. 1, 2013, federal agents entered&nbsp;
a public library in San Francisco.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.960
They arrested 29-year-old Ross Ulbricht.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.880
Ulbricht ran the largest, most sophisticated&nbsp;
online market for illegal drugs in history.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.600
He named it Silk Road, a reference to the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.520
trade routes that connected China to&nbsp;
Europe beginning in the 2nd century BC.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.280
Ulbricht hoped to create his&nbsp;
own modern-day marketplace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.440
Except his would sell hardcore&nbsp;
drugs and other illegal goods.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.480
Over the two years and ten&nbsp;
months that Silk Road operated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.700
federal prosecutors say it processed&nbsp;
nearly $214 million in sales using Bitcoin.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.388
The site operated on a hidden part of the internet
called the dark web.

00:00:42.388 --> 00:00:45.688
Prosecutors say  a journal the FBI found on Ulbricht's computer

00:00:45.688 --> 00:00:49.608
stated he wanted “to create a website where&nbsp;
people could buy anything anonymously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.608 --> 00:00:52.808
with no trail whatsoever that&nbsp;
could lead back to them.”

00:00:52.808 --> 00:00:56.568
Unfortunately for Ulbricht, he did&nbsp;
leave a trail of digital breadcrumbs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.568 --> 00:00:59.848
that would ultimately take him&nbsp;
down and his empire with it.

00:00:59.848 --> 00:01:04.408
Ulbricht was born on March&nbsp;
27, 1984, in Austin, Texas.

00:01:04.408 --> 00:01:08.808
He was a boy scout, attaining the highest rank&nbsp;
of eagle scout just like his dad had done.

00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:10.408
He had a happy childhood.

00:01:10.408 --> 00:01:15.288
Growing up an easy-going hipster but&nbsp;
serious student who scored 1460 on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.288 --> 00:01:18.568
his SATs - within the 96th percentile - and got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.568 --> 00:01:22.648
full ride to the University of Texas&nbsp;
at Dallas* (NOT Austin), where he studied physics.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:25.208
He then won another full&nbsp;
scholarship for a Master’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.208 --> 00:01:28.008
at Penn State in materials&nbsp;
science and engineering.

00:01:28.008 --> 00:01:31.368
It was at Penn that he evolved&nbsp;
into a hardcore libertarian…a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.368 --> 00:01:36.808
political philosophy that advocates individualism&nbsp;
and minimal state involvement in people’s lives.

00:01:36.808 --> 00:01:40.008
He was a fan and follower of&nbsp;
libertarian economist Ludwig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.008 --> 00:01:43.528
von Mises who opposed government&nbsp;
interference in the economy.

00:01:43.528 --> 00:01:48.008
When then-presidential candidate Mitt Romney&nbsp;
asked what is America’s greatest challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.008 --> 00:01:50.648
Ulbricht responded like&nbsp;
this on his YouTube channel.

00:01:51.688 --> 00:01:55.848
I think the most important thing is&nbsp;
getting us out of the United Nations.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:02:00.328
Ulbricht wanted to create a world free&nbsp;
from institutional or governmental control.

00:02:00.328 --> 00:02:04.968
That mindset led him to create&nbsp;
Silk Road in January 2011.

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:09.528
You couldn’t type in a regular web address to&nbsp;
get to Silk Road or use a regular web browser.

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:13.208
You needed software called Tor&nbsp;
that works as a web browser.

00:02:13.208 --> 00:02:18.008
Tor was developed by the U.S. Navy as a way&nbsp;
of communicating privately over the internet.

00:02:18.008 --> 00:02:21.128
It conceals the real IP address&nbsp;
of computers on the network to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.128 --> 00:02:24.728
hide the identity of the user and it&nbsp;
can’t be traced by the government.

00:02:24.728 --> 00:02:29.848
Silk Road’s address used a bunch of random&nbsp;
numbers and letters that ended with dot onion.

00:02:29.848 --> 00:02:32.488
Ulbricht made the site by&nbsp;
teaching himself how to code.

00:02:32.488 --> 00:02:36.648
When he needed more help he reached out&nbsp;
on a Bitcoin community forum, writing:

00:02:36.648 --> 00:02:40.408
“I'm looking for the best and brightest&nbsp;
IT pro in the bitcoin community to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.408 --> 00:02:44.168
the lead developer in a venture&nbsp;
backed bitcoin startup company.”

00:02:44.168 --> 00:02:49.368
Anyone interested was to contact him&nbsp;
via his email: rossulbricht@gmail.com

00:02:50.008 --> 00:02:54.088
Making his email public would&nbsp;
later come back to haunt him.

00:02:54.088 --> 00:02:58.168
He also got coding help from a buddy&nbsp;
of his from undergrad, Richard Bates.

00:02:58.168 --> 00:03:01.768
Ulbricht had no choice but to eventually&nbsp;
tell his friend what he was up to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.768 --> 00:03:03.528
He also told his girlfriend Julia Vie.

00:03:04.088 --> 00:03:06.168
One day, he showed her the psychedelic mushrooms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.168 --> 00:03:09.288
he was growing and selling as a&nbsp;
starter product on his new website.

00:03:09.848 --> 00:03:13.448
Silk Road would eventually be a&nbsp;
marketplace for all kinds of drugs:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.448 --> 00:03:17.448
Weed. Cocaine. LSD. Ecstasy. Heroin.

00:03:17.448 --> 00:03:19.928
This fit Ulbricht’s libertarian mindset.

00:03:19.928 --> 00:03:24.728
He believed that whatever someone decided to put&nbsp;
in their body was their choice and no one else’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.728 --> 00:03:28.008
least of all, the government’s.
 
Ulbricht also believed everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.008 --> 00:03:32.328
had the right to self-defense when guns&nbsp;
started appearing on Silk Road. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.328 --> 00:03:36.408
he realized he didn’t need the controversy&nbsp;
and soon took weapons off the site.

00:03:36.408 --> 00:03:40.808
After getting his business up and running – he&nbsp;
turned his attention to attracting customers.

00:03:40.808 --> 00:03:44.488
He decided to write a post on a Magic&nbsp;
Mushrooms forum called the Shroomery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.488 --> 00:03:47.688
pretending to be someone who&nbsp;
happened to come across Silk Road.

00:03:47.688 --> 00:03:49.966
He used the username “altoid”, posting:&nbsp;
“I'm thinking of buying off it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:50.042
but wanted to see if anyone here had&nbsp;
heard of it and could recommend it.”

00:03:50.042 --> 00:03:50.107
He included a link with instructions&nbsp;
on how to access Silk Road.

00:03:50.107 --> 00:03:50.193
I came across this website called Silk Road.&nbsp;
It's a Tor hidden service that claims to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.193 --> 00:03:51.528
allow you to buy and sell anything online&nbsp;
anonymously. I'm thinking of buying off it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.528 --> 00:03:54.968
but wanted to see if anyone here had&nbsp;
heard of it and could recommend it.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:04:02.328
He did the same on a Bitcoin site in a&nbsp;
forum about buying and selling heroin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.328 --> 00:04:05.608
describing Silk Road as “an anonymous amazon.com”

00:04:06.168 --> 00:04:08.568
It wasn’t long before buyers showed up.

00:04:08.568 --> 00:04:12.968
To limit scams, there was a rating system&nbsp;
for sellers, similar to Amazon reviews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:16.968
If a seller sold bad drugs and got a&nbsp;
poor rating, it would hurt their sales.

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:20.088
The drugs arrived by mail&nbsp;
with fake return addresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.088 --> 00:04:23.048
They’d be slipped inside CD and DVD cases.

00:04:23.048 --> 00:04:27.368
Some sellers got even more creative and&nbsp;
put them in little ripples of cardboard.

00:04:27.368 --> 00:04:30.968
The packages had printed mailing&nbsp;
labels rather than handwritten ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:33.528
to look like they came from a legitimate business.

00:04:33.528 --> 00:04:39.288
Ironically, that backfired. The printed labels&nbsp;
actually attracted the suspicion of authorities.

00:04:39.288 --> 00:04:44.568
In the summer of 2011, Department of Homeland&nbsp;
Security agent Jared Der-Yeghiayan learned of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.568 --> 00:04:49.848
small, neat package with a printed address going&nbsp;
through Chicago’s O’Hare International Airport.

00:04:49.848 --> 00:04:53.848
It contained a single pink pill of&nbsp;
ecstasy which was also suspicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.848 --> 00:04:55.928
because usually, they shipped in bulk.

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.408
Soon, two or three packages began arriving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.408 --> 00:05:04.888
then 50, then up to 1000 a day! Many came from the&nbsp;
Netherlands which is a notorious source of drugs.

00:05:04.888 --> 00:05:08.888
Agent Der-Yeghiayan visited an address where&nbsp;
one of the packages was to be delivered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.888 --> 00:05:13.368
chatted with the roommate of the buyer, who said&nbsp;
the drugs came from a site called Silk Road.

00:05:13.368 --> 00:05:16.088
Der Yeghiayan had never heard of Silk Road before.

00:05:16.088 --> 00:05:18.808
He did some digging online and&nbsp;
came across an article written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.808 --> 00:05:22.328
by Gawker journalist Adrian Chen in June 2011.

00:05:22.328 --> 00:05:25.528
Chen wrote: “Making small talk&nbsp;
with your pot dealer sucks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.528 --> 00:05:29.768
Buying cocaine can get you shot. What&nbsp;
if you could buy and sell drugs online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.768 --> 00:05:34.488
like books or light bulbs? Now&nbsp;
you can: Welcome to Silk Road.”

00:05:34.488 --> 00:05:38.648
The article attracted 3 million&nbsp;
views and put Silk Road on the map.

00:05:38.648 --> 00:05:43.448
Not only did Silk Road soon attract the&nbsp;
attention of thousands of drug dealers and buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.448 --> 00:05:48.488
but also politicians like senator Chuck Schumer&nbsp;
who called for the site to be shut down.

00:05:48.488 --> 00:05:52.408
The U.S. government was concerned&nbsp;
but not only about drug sales.

00:05:52.408 --> 00:05:55.528
As Nick Bilton detailed in&nbsp;
his book American Kingpin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.528 --> 00:06:00.408
Homeland Security agent Der-Yeghiayan feared that&nbsp;
a terrorist organization could enter the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.408 --> 00:06:03.288
and then buy something from&nbsp;
Silk Road to harm Americans.

00:06:03.288 --> 00:06:06.968
He convinced the U.S. Attorney's&nbsp;
Office in Chicago to take on the case.

00:06:06.968 --> 00:06:10.408
As Silk Road came into the spotlight,&nbsp;
Ulbricht’s college friend Richard who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.408 --> 00:06:13.608
helped him with programming said&nbsp;
he urged him to shut it down.

00:06:13.608 --> 00:06:17.688
Ulbricht and his girlfriend Julia broke&nbsp;
up soon after the launch of Silk Road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.688 --> 00:06:22.168
She said one of the reasons was because of the&nbsp;
insane pressure she felt to keep his secret.

00:06:22.728 --> 00:06:25.128
Ulbricht lied to her and Richard, telling them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.128 --> 00:06:29.128
he had sold the business to someone else&nbsp;
and no longer had anything to do with it.

00:06:29.128 --> 00:06:32.888
He moved to Australia for a while&nbsp;
and lived with his sister in Sydney.&nbsp;

00:06:32.888 --> 00:06:34.808
Around this time, he was contacted through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.808 --> 00:06:38.168
site by a person going by&nbsp;
the name “Variety Jones”.

00:06:38.168 --> 00:06:43.448
Variety Jones became his right-hand man and&nbsp;
someone Ulbricht described as a “real mentor”.

00:06:43.448 --> 00:06:45.208
Neither knew the others’ true identity.

00:06:45.928 --> 00:06:49.928
Variety Jones pointed out the gaping&nbsp;
holes in security on Silk Road.

00:06:49.928 --> 00:06:52.968
Ulbricht decided to encrypt&nbsp;
all the files on his computer.

00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:56.168
This is the actual laptop&nbsp;
he used to run Silk Road.

00:06:56.168 --> 00:06:59.368
He put in a “kill switch” that would&nbsp;
automatically shut down his device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.368 --> 00:07:03.848
by pressing a predetermined key in case&nbsp;
authorities rushed in at the last minute.

00:07:03.848 --> 00:07:06.888
He also prepared an escape&nbsp;
plan if needed, including:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.888 --> 00:07:12.568
destroy laptop, hard drive, find a place to&nbsp;
live on Craigslist for cash with a new identity.

00:07:12.568 --> 00:07:18.248
Variety Jones came up with Ulbricht’s infamous&nbsp;
pseudonym on Silk Road: Dread Pirate Roberts

00:07:18.248 --> 00:07:21.848
A reference to the fearsome captains&nbsp;
from the film The Princess Bride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.848 --> 00:07:24.568
who passed the name on to a chosen successor.

00:07:24.568 --> 00:07:26.968
In the same way, Ulbricht hoped to one day pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.968 --> 00:07:30.968
on the name Dread Pirate Roberts&nbsp;
to someone who might succeed him.

00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:34.648
Variety Jones got him to see how&nbsp;
big Silk Road could grow to be.

00:07:35.208 --> 00:07:40.408
Ulbricht wrote in a personal journal disclosed&nbsp;
by prosecutors: “Silk Road is going to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.408 --> 00:07:45.688
a phenomenon and at least one person will tell&nbsp;
me about it, unknowing that I was its creator.”

00:07:46.248 --> 00:07:52.568
In two short years, Silk Road grew to more than&nbsp;
100,000 users with sales of nearly $214 million

00:07:57.688 --> 00:07:59.848
The Feds were left scratching their heads as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.848 --> 00:08:03.128
still had no clue who was the&nbsp;
mastermind behind Silk Road.

00:08:03.128 --> 00:08:07.128
Who was this Dread Pirates Roberts?&nbsp;
Who was the captain of the ship?

00:08:07.688 --> 00:08:09.928
It became somewhat of a competition amongst the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.928 --> 00:08:14.168
various government agencies to be the&nbsp;
one to identify Dread Pirate Roberts.

00:08:14.168 --> 00:08:17.288
To better understand how Silk Road&nbsp;
operated, Department of Homeland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.288 --> 00:08:22.568
Security agent Der-Yeghiayan posed as a&nbsp;
buyer and made 52 undercover purchases.

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:24.728
He also seized thousands of packages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.728 --> 00:08:28.488
linked certain sales back to their&nbsp;
source, and arrested several people.

00:08:28.488 --> 00:08:32.488
His biggest get came when he tracked&nbsp;
down a Dread Pirate Roberts’ employee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.488 --> 00:08:36.568
a moderator on Silk Road’s&nbsp;
user forums called “cirrus”.

00:08:36.568 --> 00:08:40.168
He forced her to hand over her account&nbsp;
and then, he pretended to be her.

00:08:40.168 --> 00:08:44.888
Der-Yeghiayan posing as cirrus got assignments&nbsp;
directly from Dread Pirate Roberts.

00:08:44.888 --> 00:08:48.728
He was not the only federal agent&nbsp;
chatting with the boss of Silk Road.

00:08:48.728 --> 00:08:54.168
DEA agent Carl Force was part of a task force&nbsp;
in Baltimore that was also investigating.

00:08:54.168 --> 00:08:59.208
Force used the username “Nob” and posed as a drug&nbsp;
dealer originally from the Dominican Republic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.208 --> 00:09:04.168
who smuggled millions of dollars worth of&nbsp;
cocaine and heroin into the U.S. every year.

00:09:04.168 --> 00:09:08.648
He was on friendly terms with Dread Pirate&nbsp;
Roberts, who had no idea he was speaking with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:13.928
a DEA agent. An agent who, in a twist&nbsp;
in the tale, turned out to be corrupt.

00:09:14.568 --> 00:09:19.208
Force convinced Dread Pirate Roberts to&nbsp;
pay him $50,000 in Bitcoin by claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.208 --> 00:09:22.408
he had “insider” information&nbsp;
from a government employee.

00:09:22.408 --> 00:09:26.408
When Force reported the conversation to&nbsp;
the DEA, he claimed he never received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.408 --> 00:09:30.328
any payment when in fact, he funneled&nbsp;
the Bitcoin into a personal account.

00:09:30.888 --> 00:09:33.848
And believe it or not, a SECOND&nbsp;
agent who worked on the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.848 --> 00:09:38.968
Baltimore task force was also stealing,&nbsp;
Security Service agent Shaun Bridges.

00:09:38.968 --> 00:09:43.928
When Silk Road customer support rep Curtis&nbsp;
Clark Green was arrested at his home with a kilo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.928 --> 00:09:49.048
of coke, Bridges used Green’s admin access to&nbsp;
steal 20,000 Bitcoin from other user accounts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.048 --> 00:09:49.528
That was worth $350,000 dollars and swelled&nbsp;
to $820,000 by the time Bridges liquidated it.

00:09:49.528 --> 00:09:52.888
Dread Pirate Roberts thought Green&nbsp;
to be responsible for the theft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.888 --> 00:09:56.728
He wanted to rough him up and got&nbsp;
egged on by his mentor Variety Jones.

00:09:56.728 --> 00:10:00.488
Dread Pirate Roberts knew Green’s real&nbsp;
identity because as a condition for being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.488 --> 00:10:03.688
on Silk Road’s payroll, staff had&nbsp;
to hand over their government ID.

00:10:03.688 --> 00:10:10.728
So Dread Pirate Roberts turned to Nob (aka DEA&nbsp;
agent Carl Force) to beat up Green. Nob agreed.

00:10:10.728 --> 00:10:14.408
However, Dread Pirate Roberts then&nbsp;
changed his mind and messaged:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.408 --> 00:10:17.448
"Can you change the order to&nbsp;
execute rather than torture?"

00:10:18.248 --> 00:10:22.648
Dread Pirate Roberts said he had “never&nbsp;
killed a man or had one killed before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.648 --> 00:10:24.968
but it is the right move in this case.”

00:10:24.968 --> 00:10:28.248
He didn’t want to risk Green giving&nbsp;
up information to the authorities as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.248 --> 00:10:31.208
he knew Green had been arrested&nbsp;
when he searched him up online.

00:10:31.208 --> 00:10:34.648
Nob agreed to do the job for $80,000 in Bitcoin.

00:10:34.648 --> 00:10:37.768
Ulbricht later received photos of a dead Green.

00:10:37.768 --> 00:10:39.768
Except he wasn’t really dead.

00:10:39.768 --> 00:10:43.128
Agent Force staged Green’s death&nbsp;
complete with photos of him on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.128 --> 00:10:46.168
floor covered in Campbell’s Chicken &amp; Stars soup.

00:10:46.168 --> 00:10:51.288
The Dread Pirate Roberts is said to have ordered&nbsp;
hits on five others whom he felt threatened by.

00:10:51.288 --> 00:10:54.888
Silk Road had been prey for&nbsp;
blackmailers and extortionists.

00:10:54.888 --> 00:10:58.008
However, there was no proof&nbsp;
that anyone was ever killed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.008 --> 00:11:00.648
Ulbricht was never charged with murder for hire.

00:11:01.288 --> 00:11:06.648
The government agencies were still nowhere closer&nbsp;
to figuring out who was Dread Pirate Roberts.

00:11:06.648 --> 00:11:10.488
The DEA enlisted the help of the&nbsp;
FBI’s Cyber Crime unit in New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.488 --> 00:11:14.888
as it had more technological know-how&nbsp;
and experience with the dark web.

00:11:14.888 --> 00:11:19.048
FBI special agent Chris Tarbell knew that&nbsp;
in order to catch Dread Pirate Roberts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.048 --> 00:11:21.368
they had to wait for them to make a mistake.

00:11:21.368 --> 00:11:24.888
And according to the FBI, the&nbsp;
Dread Pirate Roberts did finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.888 --> 00:11:28.488
slip up about a year after the&nbsp;
agency started investigating.

00:11:28.488 --> 00:11:33.288
In May 2013, investigators noticed&nbsp;
coding errors - vulnerabilities on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.288 --> 00:11:36.168
the Silk Road website that leaked IP addresses.

00:11:36.168 --> 00:11:40.888
As a result, they discovered the Silk Road&nbsp;
servers were housed in a data center in Iceland.

00:11:40.888 --> 00:11:44.088
By the way, Ulbricht’s defense&nbsp;
team doesn’t buy this explanation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.088 --> 00:11:48.808
They believe the NSA spied illegally and&nbsp;
tipped off the FBI to the servers’ location.

00:11:49.368 --> 00:11:52.648
Agent Tarbell flew to Reykjavik&nbsp;
where Icelandic authorities gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.648 --> 00:11:55.288
him a drive with information from the servers.

00:11:55.288 --> 00:11:59.768
The FBI had access to a treasure trove of&nbsp;
data: They could see the number of transactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.768 --> 00:12:03.288
processed. Who logged in and out.
And crucially, Tarbell and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.288 --> 00:12:06.888
team had identified that the master&nbsp;
computer Silk Road servers talked to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.888 --> 00:12:11.608
the one Dread Pirate Roberts used to&nbsp;
log in to Silk Road, was named “Frosty”.

00:12:11.608 --> 00:12:15.928
And Dread Pirate Roberts was logging in with&nbsp;
an encryption key that ended with frosty@frosty

00:12:16.968 --> 00:12:19.928
They could also tell Dread Pirate&nbsp;
Roberts recently used internet from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.928 --> 00:12:24.408
a San Francisco cafe to log in&nbsp;
to a Silk Road server via a VPN.

00:12:24.408 --> 00:12:28.168
By the spring of 2012, Ulbright had&nbsp;
returned home from Australia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.168 --> 00:12:31.608
eventually moved to San Francisco&nbsp;
to live with a childhood friend.

00:12:31.608 --> 00:12:36.488
So now the FBI had the name of the computer&nbsp;
and could focus their search on San Francisco.

00:12:36.488 --> 00:12:40.488
But still, no idea of Dread&nbsp;
Pirate Roberts’ real identity.

00:12:40.488 --> 00:12:42.488
That piece of the puzzle would be filled in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.488 --> 00:12:46.728
by Gary Alford who worked for the&nbsp;
IRS…the Internal Revenue Service.

00:12:46.728 --> 00:12:50.568
As a tax investigator, he was assigned&nbsp;
to follow the money but instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.568 --> 00:12:53.128
discovered the identity of Dread Pirate Roberts.

00:12:53.688 --> 00:12:56.248
Alford figured that whoever&nbsp;
started Silk Road would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.248 --> 00:13:00.168
had to drum up interest in it long&nbsp;
before that Gawker article came out.

00:13:00.168 --> 00:13:04.488
So he decided to do Google searches&nbsp;
for Silk Road prior to June 2011.

00:13:04.488 --> 00:13:06.728
That’s when he stumbled on Ulbricht’s online posts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.728 --> 00:13:10.088
where he pretended to be someone who&nbsp;
happened to come across Silk Road.

00:13:10.088 --> 00:13:13.528
Alford noted the username for the posts: “altoid”.

00:13:13.528 --> 00:13:16.568
He also noticed another post&nbsp;
where “altoid” asked for IT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.568 --> 00:13:22.248
help on the Bitcoin forum which included&nbsp;
his personal email: rossulbricht@gmail.com

00:13:22.968 --> 00:13:27.768
Someone with the username Altoid also posted on&nbsp;
Stack Overflow with a question related to Tor.

00:13:28.408 --> 00:13:32.968
Soon after, that user changed&nbsp;
the alias from Altoid to Frosty.

00:13:33.688 --> 00:13:37.208
Alford didn’t know the significance&nbsp;
of the name Frosty at the time.

00:13:37.208 --> 00:13:40.328
But what he did have was&nbsp;
a real name to track down.

00:13:40.328 --> 00:13:45.048
He googled Ross Ulbricht and came across the&nbsp;
LinkedIn profile of a young man with rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.048 --> 00:13:49.528
cryptic life goals, quote: “The most&nbsp;
widespread and systemic use of force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.528 --> 00:13:54.088
is amongst institutions and governments,&nbsp;
so this is my current point of effort.”

00:13:54.088 --> 00:13:59.528
Alford asked himself: Could Ross&nbsp;
Ulbricht be the brains behind Silk Road?

00:13:59.528 --> 00:14:01.848
Then came another piece of the puzzle.

00:14:01.848 --> 00:14:06.488
In July 2013, U.S. Customs and Border&nbsp;
Protection officers intercepted nine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.488 --> 00:14:09.288
fake IDs coming into the U.S. from Canada.

00:14:09.288 --> 00:14:13.208
Homeland security agents decided to&nbsp;
pay a visit to the intended recipient.

00:14:13.208 --> 00:14:16.888
They showed up at 2260 15th&nbsp;
Avenue in San Francisco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.888 --> 00:14:18.968
the address where Ulbricht had been staying.

00:14:18.968 --> 00:14:21.608
He had moved out of the place he&nbsp;
shared with his friend and into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.608 --> 00:14:25.288
a sublet he found on Craigslist&nbsp;
where he paid his rent in cash.

00:14:25.288 --> 00:14:28.808
He took every precaution,&nbsp;
including adopting a fake identity.

00:14:28.808 --> 00:14:33.448
His roommates knew him as Joshua Terrey&nbsp;
though he kept much of his backstory the same.

00:14:33.448 --> 00:14:37.608
He said he was from Texas. Worked in IT&nbsp;
which is why he was always on his computer.

00:14:37.608 --> 00:14:40.248
And had recently returned&nbsp;
home from Australia which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.248 --> 00:14:43.048
made not having a cell phone less suspicious.

00:14:43.048 --> 00:14:46.568
“Joshua” kept to himself and&nbsp;
ate steak dinners for one.

00:14:46.568 --> 00:14:51.128
When the agents questioned Ulbricht, they&nbsp;
observed that he became “visibly nervous”. 
 &nbsp;

00:14:51.128 --> 00:14:55.048
He didn’t admit to purchasing the fake IDs&nbsp;
- let alone that he bought them so he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.048 --> 00:14:59.528
rent additional servers to deal with Silk&nbsp;
Road’s growth as well as security issues.

00:14:59.528 --> 00:15:02.488
Prosecutors later claimed he&nbsp;
prepared for a life on the run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.488 --> 00:15:05.208
including seeking citizenship&nbsp;
on the island of Dominica.

00:15:05.768 --> 00:15:09.768
Ulbricht volunteered to the agents who&nbsp;
showed up at his door that “hypothetically”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.768 --> 00:15:13.688
anyone could purchase anything they&nbsp;
wanted on a site called Silk Road.

00:15:13.688 --> 00:15:18.968
The agents had never heard of Silk Road and were&nbsp;
apparently unaware of any federal investigations.

00:15:18.968 --> 00:15:21.048
But they did document the incident.

00:15:21.048 --> 00:15:26.088
So when IRS agent Gary Alford ran another search&nbsp;
on Ulbricht, he found that Homeland Security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.088 --> 00:15:30.488
file on the fake IDs - which cemented&nbsp;
his belief that they had the right guy.

00:15:30.488 --> 00:15:34.328
The pieces of the puzzle finally came&nbsp;
together when Alford got on the phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.328 --> 00:15:36.808
with the other agencies to see what they all had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.808 --> 00:15:40.488
Alford mentioned the username&nbsp;
“Frosty” that he had found on a forum.

00:15:40.488 --> 00:15:43.928
That floored FBI agent Tarbell&nbsp;
- who knew Frosty to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.928 --> 00:15:46.728
name of the computer Dread Pirate Roberts used.

00:15:46.728 --> 00:15:50.488
Bingo. They knew Ross Ulbricht&nbsp;
was Dread Pirate Roberts.

00:15:51.128 --> 00:15:54.568
In their pursuit of evidence, federal&nbsp;
agents followed him everywhere.

00:15:54.568 --> 00:15:58.968
They also had a subpoena for Gmail and noticed&nbsp;
that whenever Dread Pirate Roberts logged into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.968 --> 00:16:03.208
Silk Road, Ulbricht was also signed into&nbsp;
his Google account. And whenever Dread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.208 --> 00:16:07.528
Pirate Roberts logged out, Ulbricht&nbsp;
also logged out of his Google account.

00:16:07.528 --> 00:16:09.848
But they knew this wouldn’t be enough to nail him.

00:16:09.848 --> 00:16:11.848
They needed concrete evidence.

00:16:11.848 --> 00:16:14.488
They had to catch Ulbright&nbsp;
while his laptop was open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.488 --> 00:16:18.008
and he was logged in on Silk&nbsp;
Road as Dread Pirate Roberts.

00:16:18.008 --> 00:16:22.248
An unlocked computer was also necessary&nbsp;
so agents could access his files.

00:16:22.248 --> 00:16:24.488
This would be difficult to pull off.

00:16:24.488 --> 00:16:29.128
Authorities knew their best bet was to catch&nbsp;
him off guard and arrest him in a public place.

00:16:29.128 --> 00:16:33.368
Homeland Security agent Jared Der-Yeghiayan was&nbsp;
still pretending to be Silk Road staff member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.368 --> 00:16:37.368
cirrus and continued to maintain&nbsp;
contact with Dread Pirate Roberts.

00:16:37.368 --> 00:16:40.888
This communication was crucial&nbsp;
to the plan they hatched.

00:16:40.888 --> 00:16:48.488
On October 1st, 2013 at 3:08 pm local time,&nbsp;
Der-Yeghiayan saw Dread Pirate Roberts log on.

00:16:48.488 --> 00:16:51.048
Ulbricht was sitting in the&nbsp;
science fiction section of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.048 --> 00:16:54.248
San Francisco public library’s Glen Park branch.

00:16:54.248 --> 00:16:58.888
Der-Yeghiayan started up a conversation with&nbsp;
Dread Pirate Roberts while posing as Cirrus.

00:16:59.608 --> 00:17:01.208
hi
are you there&nbsp;

00:17:01.848 --> 00:17:03.928
hey
how are you doing?&nbsp;

00:17:04.808 --> 00:17:08.408
i'm ok, you?
good. can you check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.408 --> 00:17:12.408
out on one of the flagged messages for me?
The flagged messages were in the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.408 --> 00:17:15.768
administrative section that only&nbsp;
Dread Pirate Roberts could access.

00:17:16.408 --> 00:17:19.368
Dread Pirate Roberts responded:
sure&nbsp;

00:17:19.368 --> 00:17:21.288
let me log in
ok&nbsp;

00:17:22.088 --> 00:17:25.128
you did bitcoin exchange before&nbsp;
you started working for me, right?&nbsp;

00:17:25.128 --> 00:17:31.448
Ulbricht asked this for security reasons because&nbsp;
only the real cirrus would know the answer.

00:17:32.088 --> 00:17:36.648
Luckily for authorities, the real Cirrus had&nbsp;
told them how to answer the security question.

00:17:36.648 --> 00:17:40.488
Agent Der-Yeghiayan hoped he&nbsp;
recalled correctly when he typed:

00:17:40.488 --> 00:17:42.328
yes, but just for a little bit

00:17:42.968 --> 00:17:44.808
It was a nerve-racking moment.&nbsp;

00:17:45.688 --> 00:17:50.328
not any more than that
no. I stopped because of reporting requirements&nbsp;

00:17:51.528 --> 00:17:55.128
damn regulators eh?
ok, which post?&nbsp;

00:17:55.128 --> 00:17:58.648
Der-Yeghiayan knew Ulbricht was&nbsp;
now looking at an admin screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.648 --> 00:18:02.808
He gave the signal to the other agents&nbsp;
positioned in the library to make a move.

00:18:02.808 --> 00:18:05.448
A commotion soon broke out near Ulbricht.

00:18:05.448 --> 00:18:09.848
A woman yelled, “F–k you” as the man&nbsp;
appeared like he was about to punch her.

00:18:09.848 --> 00:18:14.968
When Ulbricht turned around to see the fight, an&nbsp;
agent swooped in and grabbed his opened laptop.

00:18:14.968 --> 00:18:18.088
At that point, agent Der-Yeghiayan said&nbsp;
Ulbricht “lunges towards the laptop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.088 --> 00:18:20.968
And another agent walks&nbsp;
behind him - bear hugs him…”

00:18:21.528 --> 00:18:23.928
Ulbricht was arrested and taken away.

00:18:23.928 --> 00:18:28.648
The man and woman who were arguing were in fact&nbsp;
undercover agents who created a distraction.

00:18:29.608 --> 00:18:34.168
Ulbricht’s family and friends were&nbsp;
shocked. How could the kind, fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.168 --> 00:18:39.208
loving man they knew be the same person who ran&nbsp;
a global drug bazaar? They couldn’t believe it.

00:18:39.848 --> 00:18:45.208
Ulbricht was indicted on seven charges: including&nbsp;
distributing narcotics by means of the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.208 --> 00:18:48.168
and engaging in a continuing criminal enterprise.

00:18:48.168 --> 00:18:50.648
He pleaded not guilty to all charges.

00:18:51.208 --> 00:18:56.328
The trial began on January 13,&nbsp;
2015 and lasted three weeks.

00:18:56.328 --> 00:18:58.648
Many of Ulbricht’s supporters showed up…believing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.648 --> 00:19:01.448
a guilty verdict would be&nbsp;
a miscarriage of justice.

00:19:01.448 --> 00:19:04.408
In court, Ulbricht’s defense&nbsp;
attorney argued he wasn’t Dread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.408 --> 00:19:08.728
Pirate Roberts - that he had given away&nbsp;
the handle and Silk Road to someone else.

00:19:08.728 --> 00:19:10.888
But the jury didn’t buy it.

00:19:10.888 --> 00:19:17.288
On February 5, 2015, a federal jury found&nbsp;
Ross Ulbricht guilty on all seven counts.

00:19:17.288 --> 00:19:22.888
Before sentencing, Ulbricht wrote a letter to&nbsp;
the judge, begging for a more lenient sentence:

00:19:22.888 --> 00:19:28.888
“I've had my youth, and I know you must take away&nbsp;
my middle years, but please leave me my old age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.888 --> 00:19:34.008
Please leave a small light at the end of the&nbsp;
tunnel, an excuse to stay healthy, an excuse to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.008 --> 00:19:39.688
dream of better days ahead, and a chance to redeem&nbsp;
myself in the free world before I meet my maker.”

00:19:42.088 --> 00:19:48.408
On May 29, 2015, U.S. District Judge&nbsp;
Katherine B. Forrest delivered her sentence:

00:19:48.408 --> 00:19:53.528
"You were captain of the ship, as the Dread&nbsp;
Pirate Roberts, and you made your own laws and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.528 --> 00:19:58.968
you enforced those laws in the manner that you saw&nbsp;
fit. So, it wasn't a world without restriction. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.968 --> 00:20:05.288
wasn't a world of ultimate freedom. It was a world&nbsp;
of laws that you created, they were your laws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.288 --> 00:20:08.408
It is fictional to think of Silk&nbsp;
Road as some place of freedom."

00:20:08.968 --> 00:20:13.848
"No drug dealer from the Bronx selling meth&nbsp;
or heroin or crack has ever made these kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.848 --> 00:20:19.208
of arguments to the Court. It is a privileged&nbsp;
argument, it is an argument from one of privilege.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.768 --> 00:20:23.368
You are no better a person than any&nbsp;
other drug dealer and your education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.368 --> 00:20:27.368
does not give you a special place of&nbsp;
privilege in our criminal justice system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.368 --> 00:20:30.088
It makes it less explicable&nbsp;
why you did what you did."

00:20:30.648 --> 00:20:35.448
"There is no reason to make a choice between these&nbsp;
two people that I see that are on display - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.448 --> 00:20:39.608
Ulbricht who is the leader of the criminal&nbsp;
enterprise and the Ulbricht who is known and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.608 --> 00:20:45.528
loved. What is clear is that people are very,&nbsp;
very complex and you are one of them. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.528 --> 00:20:50.408
made up of many different qualities and many&nbsp;
characteristics with no one quality defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.408 --> 00:20:55.608
them. And there is good in Mr. Ulbricht,&nbsp;
I have no doubt, but there is also bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.608 --> 00:21:00.408
and what you did in connection with Silk Road&nbsp;
was terribly destructive to our social fabric.

00:21:01.048 --> 00:21:06.488
Mr. Ulbricht, it is my judgment delivered&nbsp;
here, now, on behalf of our country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.488 --> 00:21:11.768
that on Counts Two and Four you are sentenced to&nbsp;
a period of life imprisonment to run concurrently;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.568 --> 00:21:16.568
on Count Five you are sentenced to five&nbsp;
years’ imprisonment to run concurrently;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.128 --> 00:21:21.768
on Count Six, you are sentenced to 15&nbsp;
years’ imprisonment also concurrent;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.768 --> 00:21:26.888
and for money laundering in Count Seven, you&nbsp;
are sentenced to 20 years, also concurrent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.888 --> 00:21:31.688
In the federal system there is no parole&nbsp;
and you shall serve your life in prison.

00:21:34.328 --> 00:21:38.168
This was far beyond what the&nbsp;
prosecution had even asked for.&nbsp;

00:21:38.168 --> 00:21:43.048
Ulbricht had hoped Silk Road would become&nbsp;
such a success that he would be famous.

00:21:43.048 --> 00:21:46.488
According to a journal entry read&nbsp;
by the prosecutor to the jury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.488 --> 00:21:50.808
Ulbricht wrote: “I imagine that someday&nbsp;
I may have a story written about my life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.808 --> 00:21:53.368
and it would be good to have&nbsp;
a detailed account of it.”

00:21:53.368 --> 00:21:57.608
His dream came true…but perhaps&nbsp;
not in the way he had imagined.

00:21:58.568 --> 00:22:02.168
The FBI later identified&nbsp;
Ulbricht’s mentor, Variety Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.168 --> 00:22:08.408
as Roger Thomas Clark, a Canadian arrested&nbsp;
in Thailand in 2015. In 2020, Clark pled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.408 --> 00:22:13.288
guilty in a Manhattan court to conspiring to&nbsp;
distribute massive quantities of narcotics.

00:22:13.288 --> 00:22:16.648
As for the two corrupt officers&nbsp;
investigating Silk Road.

00:22:16.648 --> 00:22:21.768
Former DEA agent Carl Force was sentenced&nbsp;
to 6 ½ years in prison for extortion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.768 --> 00:22:24.568
money laundering, and obstruction of justice.

00:22:24.568 --> 00:22:28.968
Former Secret Service agent Shaun Bridges&nbsp;
was sentenced to nearly 6 years in prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.968 --> 00:22:33.448
on charges of money laundering and obstruction&nbsp;
of justice. He was later sentenced to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.448 --> 00:22:37.608
additional two years for ANOTHER theft&nbsp;
of Bitcoin from the U.S. government.

00:22:39.048 --> 00:22:43.448
This story took many, many hours of writing,&nbsp;
researching, and editing to put together.

